findings.proposed_change:
Replace lines 283-286 with:
"An oracle row result sets caused-at only for rows lost at or before the status step in the recorded run:
- recovered by R-hint: key assignment at extraction;
- by R-resolver only: key resolution or expansion;
- by R but by neither subset alone: key resolution or expansion, listed;
- by E+R but not R: extraction;
- by E+R* but not E+R: extraction (polarity);
- not by E+R*: state rules, transcript or evaluation. The exception is a case page showing a remaining wrong-modality carrier, or a positive carrier of a ruled-out value, that decided the row; that case is extraction.
An R recovery whose displaced claim the page shows to be a same-matter rival is not routing; its caused-at is set from the page.
Rows stored with the right status in the recorded run (reading losses) are outside the oracle rule. Their caused-at comes from the offline reading analysis and the page.
With neither oracle nor page evidence, caused-at is 'unclear', which counts as possible implementation. When two boundaries would each have prevented the loss, the earlier is primary."
In the E+R* row, change "What it still loses is the state rules' own share" to "what it still loses is the state rules' share, plus any remaining wrong-modality carriers and positive carriers of ruled-out values, which are listed".

findings.problem:
Steps 2, 2b and 2c run and report before Step 3a. Their outputs include per-row material:
- transition matrices per run;
- listed cases (two-key claims, newly lost rows);
- per-case probe recoveries;
- the per-question reading analysis.
The author also reviews the gates. The draft bars results only from being 'joined to a case' (pilot, line 252) or 'shown' (pass 1, line 313). Integrity F3's better_change asked for more:
- the pilot done and the sub-causes frozen 'before any oracle result is read';
- pass 1 hashed before 'any per-row oracle result' is shown;
- 'the identity-gate review sees gate results only'.
As written, pass 1 is blind on the page but not in the coder's memory, which defeats the anchoring measure and the condition-1 safeguard.

Implementation choice counts a prompt only when it 'omits information the register gives that step, or contradicts the register'. A full registry in the extraction prompt omits nothing and contradicts nothing. Neither do out-of-domain AMI role examples, and the register lists both as implementation entries. The parameter test ('excluded the right item') does not fit them either.
Yet three parts of the draft treat them as implementation causes:
- both are listed as candidate implementation causes (line 294);
- line 225 reads recovery under probes (b) and (b2) as 'evidence for an implementation-choice cause';
- the burden rule (line 274) makes any named, unexcluded alternative an implementation defect.
A coder who follows line 266 codes these cases as non-implementation; one who follows line 274 codes them as implementation. That is the coder discretion integrity F2 set out to remove, and it now sits on the largest extraction sub-causes.

The record of what was read has three problems.
- The 44% figure is misquoted. The reviewer found that about 44% of A0/A1 extraction-lost rows (119 of 271) are asked by a single-value question. Of those, 75 (about 28% of 271) had a credited RAG answer citing a gold line in at least 2 of 3 runs. The draft says 44% 'have a credited RAG answer'.
- Some reviewer results that bear on the rules are not listed:
  - the question-time check that at least 27 of 66 key_reached rows had the holding key outside the pruned prompt. This prefigures the Step 2 reading analysis, and the restriction of probe (iii) depends on it;
  - probe_r's figures (1-3 two-key claims per run, 3-12 subject-only carriers, 0-1 ties), which line 173 itself quotes;
  - the 4-of-16 same-matter keys;
  - the 12.2 of 45.2 polarity/modality losses;
  - the case counts of 55-60 per run;
  - the round-1 review itself.
- The scratch items (the fact reports, the reviewers' scripts, the ES2009 prototype) have neither a file and SHA-256 nor a statement that they are not kept (integrity F10 (e), (f)).

Step 5 (line 374) writes plan 23's verdict rule 'before these runs'. Every input to the contract and repair decisions will have been read by then:
- the A2 per-contract decomposition and the reviewer oracle (Step 2, lines 196-202);
- the D1 arm (Step 2c), whose only frozen rule is a direction ('fewer extraction losses') with no size;
- the Step 4 repair checks. A (b) repair enters the final configuration 'only under the verdict rule' (line 361).

The rule can therefore be written to admit or exclude what has been seen. It is not in 'Rules fixed by this amendment'.

The three end states also miss one case: a repair that passes its Step 4 check but is not admitted by the verdict rule. Its losses would read as 'repaired' while the final configuration still carries the defect.

Only the trade-off class is barred from retrospective text (line 268). 'Architectural weakness' needs only that 'the loss follows from a design decision of the register' (line 267). The register includes three entries written on 19 October, after cycle 1 (line 80):
- D10, which enters implementation_chapter.tex in 5de9911 and says both omissions 'surface in the evaluation';
- D11;
- the sentence that the autonomous arm leaves conflicts open.

D10 is the decision that picks short scalar claims over keeping a decision whole. So the largest extraction sub-cause, carried:claims_without_the_value (26-28 rows per run), can be coded architectural on a decision written after its losses were seen. At 94c049d, Ch. 4 discusses scalar generation only as concept granularity and calls it 'a preference of the generator', which reads as an implementation default.

Line 268 also says autonomous-arm conflicts are not a trade-off 'until the open decision on them is signed'. That invites signing the decision now, after the results, and then coding A2's 36.7 conflict rows per run as a trade-off. An examiner will check these dates.

findings.proposed_change:
Rewrite the narrower tests: 'A prompt or parameter alternative counts when any of these holds:
(i) a probe fixed in the Step 2b note recovers the case beyond the noise rule;
(ii) the prompt omits or contradicts register information;
(iii) the trace shows the parameter excluded the right item or admitted a wrong one, such as a reuse or similarity threshold merging a different matter.
Wording quality without probe evidence does not count.'

Define the outcomes now:
- recovered under (c) only, or (c) and (d) but not (b)/(b2): caused-at extraction, class unclear, which counts as possible implementation;
- model-limited: only when (d) recovers and (b), (b2) and (c) do not.

Put 'What a probe result means' into 'Rules fixed'.

findings.problem:
R-hint and R-resolver move only the gold carriers, split by the carriers' own path. A row lost to another_value_won or overwritten_by_non_gold_value is recovered once its carrier is moved away from the rival, whatever routed the rival. Plan 23 (line 252) and the 16-key sample say the rivals are mostly different matters routed onto one key.

Take a correctly hinted carrier beaten by a rival that the resolver mis-filed. R-hint recovers it, and it is mechanically coded 'key assignment at extraction'. That is the wrong boundary, and Step 4 then orders its repair by the wrong measure.

The map also leaves three cases undefined:
- recovered by R but by neither split alone;
- recovered by E+R* but not by E+R (a polarity extraction loss);
- caused-at with no oracle (A2, ES2009) and no page evidence. Round 1's 'unclear' default was dropped.

findings.proposed_change:
Replace it with a bound rule, fixed now:
- a 'doesn't work' verdict stands only if it still holds when every unrepaired-implementation and unclear loss of that mechanism is counted as recovered;
- a 'works' verdict stands only if it still holds when every pass flagged as defect-inflated is counted as lost;
- otherwise the verdict is can't tell.

Implementation then cannot serve as an excuse, and verdicts survive defects too small to change them.

findings.problem:
(1) **Coding hours.** 245 cases at 3-5 min is 12-20 h for one pass, not two. Not counted:
- the pilot (if 'next 20' is per run, another 180 cases);
- the pass examinations (credit_without_state plus about 7 pass rows per run, about 90-100 items);
- a fresh sample after a kappa shortfall;
- the alternatives that the burden rule requires each page to write up.

(2) **Probe cost.** Probe (i) covers 152 extraction-lost cases (22-27 per A0/A1 run) at about 8k input tokens per recorded prompt. That is about $11-13, of which (d) is about $4, so it is not 'almost all'. Probe (ii) re-resolves about 150 carrier texts plus their rivals, three times, at full-registry prompts, which adds roughly $7-10. The total is about $20. The adjudication of about 2,000 probe outputs is not covered by '1-2 days'.

(3) **Spend.** $32 plus $35-65 gives $67-97, which is inside $60-100, so 'would go past that range' does not follow. But Step 4's $20 is per LLM-seam repair, so the true upper end is higher.

